{
 "id": "2020-vassiljev-urban",
 "name": "Urban Blue Acupuncture: Waterside Design Options (rating + favourite)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Vassiljev, Bell, Balicka & Amrita (2020), Sustainability 12:10656 (EU BlueHealth project). Participants imagine living near a shore at Kopli bay, Tallinn, and see nine virtual designs for getting closer to the water: three for a steep cliff, three for a sandy beach, three for reed beds. They see the designs one at a time in random order. For each they answer ten 1–5 questions (time they would spend, comfort, view, closeness to nature, safety, relaxation, social time, wildlife, water contact, accessibility), say whether they would return (yes/no) and can add a comment. They then pick their most and least favourite design and answer Inclusion of Nature in Self. Wording follows the paper's Table 2 and the English questionnaire in Supplement B. In the original survey (252 Estonian residents), Beach 3 and Beach 1 were by far the favourites.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) each design is shown as a still render from the paper (Figures 8–16), not the animated walk-through video. Likewise the intro video is replaced by the shore view in Figure 6. (2) English only; the original was offered in Estonian and Russian. (3) The most/least favourite choice shows labelled thumbnails, and the labels use the paper's option names. (4) The Inclusion of Nature in Self diagrams are redrawn after Schultz (2001).\n\nImages: VR model renders by Peeter Vassiljev from the paper (CC BY 4.0). Used with credit for research; the template is removed if the authors object.",
